### Why Java 8 Changed the Interface Rules

Prior to Java 8, interfaces could **only** declare abstract methods. If Java architects wanted to add a new method like `stream()` or `forEach()` to `java.util.Collection`, every single class in the world implementing `Collection` (thousands of enterprise libraries) would fail compilation!

To solve this, Java 8 introduced **default methods**:
```java
public interface Collection<E> {
    // Added in Java 8 with a default body! Zero broken legacy classes!
    default void forEach(Consumer<? super E> action) {
        for (E e : this) action.accept(e);
    }
}
```

### 1. Default Method Rules
- Declared using the `default` keyword inside an interface.
- Must provide an implementation body `{ ... }`.
- Implementing classes inherit the default body automatically, but can **optionally override** it.
- Default methods can access other abstract or default methods declared in the interface.

### 2. Resolving the Multiple Inheritance Conflict
If a class implements two interfaces that declare the **exact same default method signature**, the compiler refuses to guess which one to use. The class MUST override the method and choose:
```java
interface A { default void log() { System.out.println("A"); } }
interface B { default void log() { System.out.println("B"); } }

class App implements A, B {
    @Override
    public void log() {
        // Disambiguate using: InterfaceName.super.methodName()
        A.super.log(); // Delegates to A's default
    }
}
```

### 3. Static Methods in Interfaces
Java 8 also allowed `static` methods in interfaces. They serve as utility or factory helpers bound to the interface namespace (`InterfaceName.utilityMethod()`), and are **NOT inherited** by implementing classes.

